        "heading": "1 INTRODUCTION",
        "text": "Unlike herds or swarms, human social networks solve different tasks with different social network structures (Momennejad, 2022). Human and computational studies show that, to optimize the performance of collective learning by choosing who should communicate with whom, one needs to take into account the inter-play between task and network properties: networks with high connectivity are better suited for quick convergence in problems with clear global optima (Coman et al., 2016; Momennejad et al., 2019) while partially-connected structures perform best in deceptive tasks, where acting greedily in the short-term leads to missing the optimal trajectory (Derex & Boyd, 2016; Lazer & Friedman, 2007; Cantor et al., 2021). Despite this evidence, works in decentralized reinforcement learning (RL) have not to date considered the effect of group connectivity, with most solutions adopting a fully-connected architecture with a single learner and multiple actors (Mnih et al., 2016; Horgan et al., 2018; Espeholt et al., 2018; Nair et al., 2015; Christianos et al., 2020; Schmitt et al., 2019). Here, we test the performance of different social network structures in groups of RL agents that share their individual experiences. We refer to such entities as multi-agent topologies, introduce SAPIENS, an algorithm for Structuring multi-Agent toPology for Innovation through ExperieNce Sharing1, and evaluate it in a type of deceptive task that models collective innovation.\n1We provide an implementation of SAPIENS and code to reproduce the simulations we report in https: //anonymous.4open.science/r/SAPIENS-859E.\nInnovations represent the expansion of an agent\u2019s behavioral repertoire with new problem-solving abilities and are, therefore, a necessary ingredient of continuous learning and exploration (Leibo et al., 2019). The solitary genius narrative often obscures the collective nature of human innovations, which arise from tinkering, recombination and adoption of existing innovations (Sole\u0301 et al., 2013; Derex & Boyd, 2016). Cognitive and ecological studies of human technological innovation characterize it as a type of combinatorial search constrained by semantics that describe which combinations of innovations are feasible (Sole\u0301 et al., 2013; Derex & Boyd, 2016). Here, we adopt this definition, so that innovation tasks: a) exhibit a multi-level search space, where innovations arise out of recombination of existing ones(Hafner, 2021) b) are characterized by rewards that increase monotonically with the level of innovation. This property captures the human intrinsic motivation for progress (Sole\u0301 et al., 2013) and contributes to the deceptive nature of these tasks, attracting agents toward potentially sub-optimal paths.\nLaboratory and field studies of human groups have shown that collective innovation is highly contingent on the social network structure (Momennejad, 2022; Migliano & Vinicius, 2022; Derex & Boyd, 2016). The reason for this lies in the exploration versus exploitation dynamics of social networks: partial connectivity leads to high clustering and long shortest path, which helps maintain diversity in the collective at the benefit of exploration. In contrast, high-connectivity quickly leads to conformity which benefits exploitation (Lazer & Friedman, 2007). Of particular interest are social network structures that achieve a balance in this trade-off: small-worlds are static graphs that, due a modular structure with long-range connections, achieve both high clustering and small shortest path Watts & Strogatz (1998). Another example are dynamic structures, where agents are assigned to distinct groups but are able to periodically visit other groups (Volz & Meyers, 2007). These graphs have the attractive property that they both locally protect innovations, thus maintaining diversity, and allow sharing so that agents can recombine the solutions of the collective and discover the global optimum quickly in large search spaces (Derex & Boyd, 2016).\nIn this paper we study the effect of social network structure in multi-agent topologies that solve innovation tasks. We propose SAPIENS, an algorithm connecting DQN learners (Mnih et al., 2013) that independently explore the environment and share experience tuples from their replay buffers with their neighbors in different static and dynamic social network structures. We employ Wordcraft (Jiang et al., 2020) as a test-bed and design three custom tasks (Figures 1 and 2) covering innovation challenges of different complexity: (i) a task with a single innovation path to an easy-to-find global optimum. This type of task can be used to model a linear innovation structure, such as the evolution of the fork from knife to having, two-, three- and, eventually four tines. (Sole\u0301 et al., 2013) (ii) a task with two paths that individually lead to local optima, but when combined, can merge toward the global optimum. With this task we can capture innovations that were repurposed after their invention, such as Gutenberg\u2019s screw press leading to the print press (Sole\u0301 et al., 2013). (iii) a task with ten paths, only one of which leads to the global optimum, which captures search in vast spaces.. In addition to Wordcraft, we also evaluate SAPIENS on a deceptive task implemented in a grid world.\nWe empirically show that the performance of SAPIENS depends on the inter-play between social network structure and task demands. Dynamic structures perform most robustly, exhibiting an impressive ability to converge quickly in the easy task, avoid local optima in the second task, and explore efficiently in the third task. We propose and compute measures of conformity and diversity (Momennejad et al., 2019) to interpret these findings and compare the performance of SAPIENS with other decentralized RL algorithms (Mnih et al., 2016; Nair et al., 2015).\nContributions Our contributions are two-fold. From a neuroscience and cognitive science perspective, SAPIENS is, to our knowledge, the first computational study that attempts at reproducing results observed in human groups on the effect of social network structure on collective innovation, that employs deep RL as the individual learning mechanism. Compared to previous computational studies that employed simple agent-based models and evolutionary search (Lazer & Friedman, 2007; Cantor et al., 2021; Mason & Watts, 2012), deep RL provides three main advantages : i) it enables empirical experiments with more complex test-beds and larger search spaces ii) agents can share their experience by simply exchanging transitions from their respective replay buffers, without requiring ad-hoc mechanisms for copying the behaviors of other agents iii) by using the replay buffer as a proxy of the memories of agents, we can directly measure properties such as diversity and alignment of experiences that are challenging to estimate with human studies. From an RL perspective, our work extends upon the decentralized RL paradigm by systematically analyzing the\neffect of different social network structures on different class of tasks. We propose novel metrics enabling a thorough analysis of the learning dynamics. In summary, our contributions provide a better understanding of results originally obtained in human experiments and build upon these insights to propose a novel algorithm for distributed RL.\nRelated work Experience sharing among RL agents can significantly improve both convergence rate and performance (Horgan et al., 2018; Mnih et al., 2016; Schmitt et al., 2019; Nair et al., 2015). Shared experience may take the form of experience tuples or gradients, the former being common among off-policy algorithms (Souza et al., 2019; Horgan et al., 2018; Mnih et al., 2016) and baring benefits over sharing gradients in actor-critic architectures, where instability and latency are concerns (Schmitt et al., 2019; Christianos et al., 2020). For instance, the Gorila framework (Nair et al., 2015), introduced a general multi-learner architecture with both local and global replay buffers, but focused on parameter sharing rather than experience sharing. A common architecture for experience sharing is that of multiple actors and a single learner (Mnih et al., 2016; Horgan et al., 2018; Schmitt et al., 2019). Another approach included experience sharing between multiple actors/learners but the groups only included two agents (Souza et al., 2019)."
